<h2>A night in February 1996</h2>
<p>During the night of 2-3 February 1996, Dheli Devi was found murdered in her house at Kalyanpura, with jewellery missing. Two men — Jagia @ Jagdish and Khetiya — were arrested on 20 March 1996. The investigation assembled a circumstantial case: Jagia&rsquo;s left-foot impression matched one lifted from the scene; at his instance a blood-stained knife, shirt and trousers were recovered, on which the FSL found blood of group A, matching the deceased&rsquo;s articles; a gold ring of the deceased was recovered from Jagia and a pendant from Khetiya, both identified by the deceased&rsquo;s adopted son.</p>

<h2>The long route to judgment</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1997:</strong> Trial court convicts — Jagia under Sections 302 and 380 IPC; Khetiya under Section 380 IPC.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2004:</strong> The High Court allows the appeal and acquits both accused.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Supreme Court:</strong> The matter is carried up, and the Supreme Court remands the appeal to the High Court for fresh decision.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2026:</strong> On remand, the Division Bench re-hears and dismisses the appeal, restoring finality to the 1997 convictions.</div>
</div>

<h2>The chain holds</h2>
<p>Applying the settled framework for circumstantial cases, the Bench held that the cumulative circumstances — presence established by the footprint, the recoveries stained with the deceased&rsquo;s blood group, and recent unexplained possession of her jewellery — formed a complete chain pointing only to the accused. On the jewellery, the court invoked <em>Earabhadrappa v. State of Karnataka</em>, AIR 1983 SC 446: recent and unexplained possession of a murdered person&rsquo;s belongings raises the presumption under Section 114, Illustration (a) of the Evidence Act, and delay in apprehension did not break the chain (paras 17-19, 27). The court recorded that &ldquo;the defence has not brought on record any reasonable explanation capable of breaking this chain&rdquo; (para 21). The defence citations — including authorities on weak recoveries and incomplete chains — did not persuade the Bench on these facts.</p>

<h2>What a Supreme Court remand means in practice</h2>
<div class="note">
<p>An Article 136 remand does not dictate the result; it reopens the appellate exercise. The High Court on remand re-appreciates the entire record, free to acquit or convict afresh. Here, the same record that produced a 2004 acquittal produced a 2026 affirmance — a sobering illustration that in circumstantial cases, outcomes can turn on how a bench weighs the same links. Accused persons who win in the High Court should treat a State SLP seriously: a remand places everything back on the table, decades later and often with worsened practical circumstances for the defence.</p>
</div>

<h2>Lessons for circumstantial-evidence appeals</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Recoveries plus serology remain potent: blood-group matching on weapons and clothing, though less precise than DNA, continues to anchor convictions from the pre-DNA era.</li>
  <li>Possession of the deceased&rsquo;s valuables demands an explanation under Section 313 CrPC; silence or denial leaves the Earabhadrappa presumption operating at full strength.</li>
  <li>Footprint evidence, often treated as weak in isolation, gains force as one link among several — the chain is judged cumulatively, not link by link.</li>
  <li>Old appeals do not fade. Legal teams should preserve records, trace witnesses and plan for the possibility of remand-era rehearing, where the paper record is effectively all that remains.</li>
</ul>
</div>
